        "abstract": "In this work, the differences in the trap-ping/detrapping characteristics of Metal-Gate/High-k/III-V MOS stacks is experimentally studied by means of the C-V Hysteresis and dynamic stress. Samples under study include the combination of n-InP and n-InGaAs substrates with HfO<sub xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">2</sub> or Al<sub xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">2</sub>O<sub xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">3</sub> dielectrics as gate oxides. This allows to assess the impact of both the substrate and the dielectric on the quality of the complete structure. Results show that Al<sub xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">2</sub>O<sub xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">3</sub>-based stacks exhibit lower overall trapped charge during hysteresis cycles than their HfO<sub xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">2</sub> counterparts. Additionally, InP-based samples introduce a larger amount of defects above the fermi-level when compared to InGaAs samples for positive stress, but with negligible trapping effects when stressing towards inversion, which is a positive indicator in terms of reliability.",
